{% set local_problem %}
<p>Scottsboro properties face some unique drainage challenges. Clay soil doesn't absorb water well, so surface-deposited water tends to pool and find its way toward foundations. Lake-area properties often sit on sloped lots where water naturally runs back toward the house.</p>

<p>Surface downspout extensions help, but they only move water a few feet — often not enough to solve serious drainage problems. For properties with persistent wet basements, foundation concerns, or challenging topography, underground drains provide a permanent solution.</p>

<p>For vacation homeowners, underground drains have an added benefit: they work automatically with zero maintenance. Your drainage system protects your property whether you're there or not.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes that connect to your downspouts and carry water away from your home through a sealed, sloped channel. The water exits at a predetermined point far from your foundation.</p>

<p>We use 4-inch corrugated or PVC pipe, depending on the application. The system is designed with proper slope (typically 1-2% grade) to ensure water flows away rather than pooling. Pop-up emitters or daylight exits release water at the discharge point.</p>

<p>Unlike French drains (which collect groundwater), underground gutter drains specifically handle roof runoff. They work with your gutter system to create complete water management from roof to safely away from your home.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've installed underground drainage systems throughout Jackson County for over 20 years. We understand Scottsboro's soil conditions, the unique challenges of lakeside lots, and how to design systems that solve problems permanently.</p>

<p>Our systems are built to last decades with minimal maintenance — exactly what Scottsboro vacation homeowners need.</p>
{% endset %}
